# Domain redirect for minicycleapp.com (with forced redirect)
https://minicycleapp.com/*  https://minicycle.app/  301!
https://www.minicycleapp.com/*  https://minicycle.app/  301!

# Docs subdomain (own origin — Docsify's multi-MB localStorage search index
# must not share the app origin's ~5MB quota; see netlify.toml DOCS SUBDOMAIN
# block for the full story). Forced: must beat static files and the
# host-agnostic root/catch-all rules below for the docs host.
https://docs.minicycle.app/*  /docs/:splat  200!
https://minicycle.app/docs/*  https://docs.minicycle.app/:splat  301!

# Legacy URL redirects (preserve old links)
/privacy.html           /legal/privacy.html           301
/terms.html             /legal/terms.html             301
/user-manual.html       /legal/user-manual.html       301
/product.html           /                             301
# The product page lives at the site root (Nov 2026, the app moved to /app).
# Netlify's pretty URLs also serve it at /pages/product (no extension), so send
# that twin to the root. FORCED (301!): pretty-URL resolution matches the file
# first and an unforced rule is never consulted (measured live after v2.593).
# /pages/product.html itself is NOT redirected: the root rewrite below serves
# that file, and a redirect on a rewrite's own target risks a loop. It still
# answers 200, with its canonical / og:url naming the root.
/pages/product          /                             301!
/learn_more.html        /pages/learn_more.html        301
/miniCycle-lite.html    /lite/miniCycle-lite.html     301
/miniCycleGames/*       /games/:splat                 301

# Short, citable alias for the .mcyc format spec. A format other people are
# invited to implement needs a URL that survives being pasted into a README.
# MUST sit above the /* SPA fallback below — that rule answers every unmatched
# path with miniCycle.html and a 404 status, which is what swallowed this alias
# when it was (wrongly) declared in netlify.toml instead of here. Path redirects
# live in THIS file; netlify.toml carries the headers.
/mcyc                   /pages/mcyc-format.html       301

# Short alias for the Lite app (old-device fallback). Both spellings: without
# the slash Netlify would otherwise hand the bare path to the SPA fallback,
# with it the directory has no index.
/lite                   /lite/miniCycle-lite.html     301
/lite/                  /lite/miniCycle-lite.html     301

# The app (Nov 2026: moved from the root, which is now the product page).
# /app is the address; the old app addresses lead to it. /minicycle is the
# pretty URL Netlify gives miniCycle.html (and /miniCycle.html 301s to it), so
# FORCED to beat the file. NO "/app/ → /app" rule: Netlify matches /app and
# /app/ as one path, so it would redirect /app to itself forever. The page
# fixes the slash itself (step 0 of miniCycle.html's head gauntlet).
/minicycle   /app               301!
/app         /miniCycle.html    200

# The site root is the product page.
/    /pages/product.html    200

# Handle 404s by serving miniCycle.html (SPA fallback)
/*   /miniCycle.html    404
